"Exodus 20:3-6 and Deuteronomy 4:15-19; 5:7-10 prohibit other gods and image worship, and prophets such as Isaiah 44 satirize idol-making. Tractate Avodah Zarah defines forbidden acts (bowing, sacrificing, incense, libation) and restricts commerce near idolatrous festivals. Studying ancient religions as history is distinct from worship. Medieval authorities differed on whether Christianity counts as idolatry, with Meiri taking lenient positions, while Maimonides held that Islam is strictly monotheistic.",
